body: "## Plain replay skips execution-refusal integrity validation\n\n**Hosted reproduced regression plus exact-source evidence**, PR #39 head `a59ee77cfa3e57c5bd6ac4d49d8b066f25b4138c`. This is not a claim about a published package or deployed consumer.\n\n`replayRlm` removed unconditional `validateRecorded()` and now invokes it only when `state.components` or `state.reuse_identity` is present. Ordinary runs have neither. Their traversal skips recorded refusals without comparing their code/reason to the actual action outcome.\n\n- [Current replay entry point](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rlm/blob/a59ee77cfa3e57c5bd6ac4d49d8b066f25b4138c/src/engine.ts#L480-L524)\n- [Existing regression fixture](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rlm/blob/a59ee77cfa3e57c5bd6ac4d49d8b066f25b4138c/test/review-r6.test.ts#L38-L70)\n- [Node 22 failure](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rlm/actions/runs/40749366895/job/123031608027)\n- [Node 26 failure](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rlm/actions/runs/40749366895/job/123031608556)\n\nReproduction on the stated source head, after its normal dependency setup: `node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern=\"review r6 replay rejects altered aggregate and read execution refusals\" test/review-r6.test.ts`. This uses the existing synthetic fixture; no external model is needed.\n\nThe existing `review r6 replay rejects altered aggregate and read execution refusals` test uses a real SDK scratch tracker and deterministic model, creates a successful plain run with sum/get refusals, replaces a saved refusal reason/code through the checkpoint fixture, then expects replay rejection before inference or history writes. Both jobs fail with `Missing expected rejection.` Related existing legacy/spawn/scope refusal-tampering tests fail the same way. Resume counterparts pass; this finding is specifically the read-only replay validation boundary. Both jobs report 462/485 tests passing; 23 failures include subtests and their containing parent, not 23 independent product defects.\n\nExpected: ordinary replay authenticates execution-refusal records as well as successful reads and final answers, without extra model calls or tracker writes. Actual: a forged refusal can be accepted in a successful plain replay. This weakens durable-history verification and means successful replay no longer attests the refusal transcript promised by existing tests.\n\nThe [current accepted implementation record](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-rlm/blob/a59ee77cfa3e57c5bd6ac4d49d8b066f25b4138c/.agents/pm/features/pm-rlm-repair-runs.toon) includes native replay in the shared-boundary audit and explicitly retains old assertions/thresholds. Preserving historical read-only compatibility should not bypass refusal verification.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Restore equivalent execution-refusal verification for plain replay, preserving legitimate legacy placeholder, read and accounting compatibility.\n- Cover both plain and component/reuse-identity modes so stronger opt-in validation does not weaken the default path.\n- Reject forged code, reason, missing modern reason and spawn/refusal provenance before a successful result; preserve unchanged source history and zero inference.\n- Pass existing review-r6/r7/r8 and evidence/spawn regressions unchanged, with fixed/reverted/restored proof and final-head Node22/26 gates.\n- Keep separate proof-target maintenance (#38) and SDK dead-writer recovery (unbraind/pm-cli#1609) independent.\n\nLimits: independently inspected hosted exact-head logs/source, not a new local end-to-end SDK or crash/concurrency reproduction. All 41 all-status issue/PR records and current PR conversation/review records checked; no existing runtime issue matches. Related #38 covers behavioral proof targeting, not this returned-success validation gap.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-10-28T19:13:04Z)\n>\n> <!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n> \n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-rlm#15 - feat: certify answer evidence coverage per declared component [merged]\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\":\"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
